    "terminal_reason": "completed",
    "result_text": "I implemented `search_remote` in `kb/remote.py`. It connects to the server over Streamable HTTP, calls the `search` tool and returns titles through the same `split_titles` that `search_local` uses.\n\n**How the token is sent:** in the pinned mcp 2.2.0, `streamable_http_client(url, http_client=...)` no longer takes a `headers` argument, and it uses `httpx2`, not `httpx`. So I create an `httpx2.AsyncClient` with the `Authorization: Bearer <token>` header set on the client and pass it in. The transport sends every request through that client, so the header goes on all of them. `httpx2` comes with mcp, so no dependency was added.\n\n**Timeouts:** I set a 30s timeout, with a 300s read timeout, to match what the SDK sets on the clients it builds itself. Without it, the client's default read timeout could cut off long-lived response streams. I copied the values rather than calling the SDK's helper, because the helper is in a private module (`mcp.shared._httpx_utils`).\n\n**Verification:**\n- `pytest -q`: 2 passed. Neither test covers `search_remote`.\n- I also ran a live check: a real MCP server on localhost behind a wrapper that returns 401 to any request without the right bearer token. `search_remote` returned `['Title A for vpn', 'Title B']`. All six HTTP requests (the POSTs, the GET stream and the closing DELETE) carried `Bearer s3cret`.\n\n**Scratch file to delete:** the check script `_e2e_kb.py` is still in the project root, because my `rm` needed approval. Please delete it, or allow the `rm` and I'll do it."
  },
